  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nEarly this morning, a upper level shortwave is rounding the upper \nlevel ridge over the Plains. This shortwave is moving into the local \narea providing enough lift to produce weak returns on radar moving \ninto the eastern Kansas and western Missouri. These returns are \nfalling into dry air below 10kft which is keeping any precipitation \nrelegated to brief very light sprinkles. These brief sprinkles may \ncontinue until mid-morning when the upper shortwave will weaken and \nsurface high pressure builds into the area from the east. Despite \nsurface high pressure providing weak mixing today, height rises \nacross the area due to the upper level ridge becoming more amplified \nwill drive highs into the low to mid 70s. Continued height rises are \nexpected Thursday as the upper level ridge axis moves from the \ncentral Plains to the eastern Plains in response to a upper level \ntrough moving into the California coast. This will drive highs into \nthe mid to upper 70s. Friday and Saturday, the upper level ridge \naxis moves directly over the area providing further height rises \ncoupled with modest WAA. This will aid in highs rising into the mid \n70 to near 80. \n\nBy Sunday, the aforementioned upper level trough will move from the \nGreat Basin to the Eastern Rockies weakening as it does. This will \nforce an attendant cold front into the Plains on Sunday. The local \narea will remain in the warm sector ahead of the front with highs \nagain in the 70s. Monday, weak trough will move into the central \nPlains finally forcing the cold front into the CWA providing the \nnext chance for precipitation. The upper trough will then move \nthrough the region on Monday night continuing shower chances as the \nfront is forcing through the CWA. Temperatures in Monday will range \nfrom the lower 60s to lower 70s. Surface high pressure will build \ninto the area behind the cold front cooling highs to near seasonal \nnormals in the upper 50s to mid 60s.",
